  "text": "IXELM, Please help me\nLet's solve the problem step by step.\n\nGiven the system of equations:\n1. \\(|25 + 20i - z| = 5\\)\n2. \\(|z - 4 - k| = |z - 3i - k|\\)\n\nWe need to find the values of \\(k\\) such that the system has exactly one complex solution \\(z\\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the equations in terms of real and imaginary parts. Let \\(z = x + yi\\), where \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are real numbers. Then the equations become:\n\n1. \\(\\sqrt{(25 + 20i - x - yi)^2} = 5\\)\n2. \\(\\sqrt{(x + yi - 4 - k)^2} = \\sqrt{(x + yi - 3i - k)^2}\\)\n\nThis simplifies to:\n1. \\(\\sqrt{(25 - x - 20i - yi)^2} = 5\\)\n2. \\(\\sqrt{(x - 4 - k + yi)^2} = \\sqrt{(x - 3i - k + yi)^2}\\)\n\nSince the magnitudes are equal, we can square both sides:\n1. \\((25 - x - 20i - yi)^2 = 25\\)\n2. \\((x - 4 - k + yi)^2 = (x - 3i - k + yi)^2\\)\n\nLet's solve the first equation:\n\\[\n(25 - x - 20i - yi)^2 = 25\n\\]\n\\[\n(25 - x - (20 + y)i)^2 = 25\n\\]\nThis implies that the real part is zero and the imaginary part is zero. So we have:\n\\[\n25 - x = 0 \\implies x = 25\n\\]\n\\[\n-(20 + y)i = 0 \\implies y = -20\n\\]\nSo, \\(z = 25 - 20i\\).\n\nNow, let's substitute \\(z = 25 - 20i\\) into the second equation:\n\\[\n|25 - 20i - 4 - k| = |25 - 20i - 3i - k|\n\\]\n\\[\n|21 - 20i - k| = |25 - 23i - k|\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(21 - k - 20i)^2} = \\sqrt{(25 - k - 23i)^2}\n\\]\nSince the magnitudes are equal, we have:\n\\[\n21 - k - 20i = 25 - k - 23i \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 21 - k - 20i = -(25 - k - 23i)\n\\]\nThe first equation simplifies to:\n\\[\n-20i = -23i \\implies i = 0\n\\]\nThis is not possible, so we discard this equation. The second equation simplifies to:\n\\[\n21 - k - 20i = -25 + k + 23i\n\\]\n\\[\n46 = 2k + 43i\n\\]\nSince the real part is zero, we have:\n\\[\n2k = 46 \\implies k = 23\n\\]\nSo, the only possible value of \\(k\\) is 23. Therefore, the sum of all possible values of \\(k\\) is 23, and the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{23}\n\\]",
